Fear of Disclosure / Outing Anxiety - Social Anxiety Disorder (or Specific Phobia) - V62.89 (Target of perceived adverse discrimination)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Accidental discovery anxiety, Meticulous self-censoring, Extreme compartmentalization

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-016
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of autonomic panic and paranoia resulting in complete functional paralysis and refusal to leave the home.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute episode of severe diaphoresis, tachycardia, and overwhelming panic triggered by a perceived privacy breach, requiring urgent face-to-face assessment to rule out an acute cardiac event or progression to acute psychosis prior to routine GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I feel like I'm having a heart attack, I can't breathe, and if anyone finds out who I really am, my life is over.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a multi-year history of constant low-grade background anxiety regarding accidental discovery of their identity, leading to meticulous self-censoring of speech and personal history in public spaces. Over the past 24 hours, following an unexpected question about their background from a colleague, the patient experienced an acute panic response characterized by extreme compartmentalization of social spheres completely collapsing into chronic paranoia regarding digital footprints and data tracking. The patient reports severe emotional exhaustion from maintaining complex fabrications and profound reluctance to seek appropriate medical care out of fear of documenting their marginalized status. This culminated tonight in somatic symptoms including excessive sweating, tachycardia, and an inability to leave the physical residence.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; confined to home due to severe panic and paranoia.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of profound social isolation, occupational loss, and medical neglect due to extreme avoidance behaviors.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 | HR: 112 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears acutely distressed, diaphoretic, hypervigilant, pacing the room, and repeatedly checking window blinds.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, regular rhythm, no murmurs. Strong peripheral pulses. Respiratory: Tachypneic, clear breath sounds bilaterally, no wheeze. Neurological: Alert and fully oriented. Cranial nerves intact. Noticeable fine tremor in bilateral hands. The clinical presentation is dominated by accidental discovery anxiety and meticulous self-censoring during the interview (e.g., pausing extensively before answering basic demographic questions). Extreme compartmentalization is evident; the patient has multiple separate phones on the table and expresses terror at them ringing simultaneously.
Environmental Observations: All blinds are drawn, doors are deadbolted, and multiple electronic devices are visibly turned off or covered.
Medication Reconciliation: No current prescription medications. Denies illicit substance use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Fear of Disclosure / Outing Anxiety - Social Anxiety Disorder (or Specific Phobia) - V62.89 (Target of perceived adverse discrimination) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to absence of radiating chest pain, normal oxygen saturation, and symptoms being explicitly triggered by a social/psychological event rather than physical exertion.

Stimulant Intoxication - Ruled out/unlikely based on patient history, absence of pupillary dilation, and the specific, context-dependent nature of the paranoia.

Primary Psychotic Disorder (e.g., Schizophrenia) - Ruled out/unlikely as the paranoia is strictly circumscribed to the realistic social threat of disclosure and discrimination, without bizarre delusions or auditory hallucinations.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe autonomic hyperarousal, including resting tachycardia (HR 112), elevated blood pressure (148/92), and profound diaphoresis driven by a severe social panic response. Immediate, out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to physically examine the patient, definitively exclude a dangerous cardiac arrhythmia or ischemic event, and provide immediate pharmacological down-regulation of the nervous system. Delaying care until routine morning hours would have left the patient in an unsafe state of severe panic, potentially escalating to a hypertensive crisis or psychiatric emergency, thus meeting the criteria for urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral propranolol 10mg to manage the acute somatic symptoms of panic (tachycardia and tremor). Recommended the usual GP order an evaluation of baseline sympathetic tone and a psychometric evaluation showing high public self-consciousness and hypervigilance.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the acute accidental discovery anxiety, practice the "5-4-3-2-1 Grounding Technique" immediately upon feeling the physical signs of panic; successfully redirecting sensory input will positively reinforce your ability to control the physical anxiety response. To address the exhaustion of extreme compartmentalization, schedule a single, 15-minute window daily to review privacy settings or digital footprints, physically closing the laptop afterward; enforcing this boundary reduces chronic vigilance and rewards the brain with dedicated rest time.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized, culturally competent clinical psychologist.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours to monitor blood pressure, review the efficacy of the beta-blocker, and establish a safe, long-term therapeutic relationship.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing crushing central chest pain, shortness of breath at rest not relieved by grounding, or the emergence of active suicidal thoughts.

